    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, i think you meant high-acid as in low pH, not low-acid. my bad. so if we're dealing with a low pH environment, how does the lecithin's amphiphilic structure actually prevent the oil droplets from coalescing? i'm trying to understand the electrostatic repulsion aspect specifically.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a low pH environment, the electrostatic repulsion caused by negatively charged lecithin molecules helps prevent oil droplets from coalescing. The negatively charged phosphate groups (on the sn-1 position of the phospholipid) can attract positively charged polar lipids or other negatively charged lecithin molecules, while also repelling non-polar oil droplets due to their similar negative charge. This electrostatic interaction increases the repulsions between adjacent oil droplets, reducing coalescence and maintaining the emulsion.",
