      "content": "The loss of emulsions over time is often attributed to Ostwald ripening, a thermodynamically driven process. In this case, tiny droplets of emulsifier or stabilizer, like lecithin enzymes, can act as volatile emulsifiers that are more easily removed than anchored ones. The constant supply of these volatile emulsifiers from lecithinase promotes their release from the emulsion, where they can newly stabilize and reform larger oil droplets, but ultimately, the system tends towards equilibrium via phase inversion.",
